<description>
Recolor every red-green domino to a teal pixel at the green cell and erase the red cell.

Input structure
- The grid is a black-background scene containing red pixels, green pixels, and grey marker pixels.
- Some red and green pixels form isolated 4-connected two-cell dominoes; other red/green pixels are unpaired distractors.
Rule
- For each 4-connected red-green domino, set the green pixel to teal and set the red pixel to the background color.
- Preserve grey pixels and all unpaired red/green pixels.
Key insight
- Only orthogonal red-green contact matters: the green side survives as the new teal marker while the red side is deleted.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "4-connected red-green dominoes on a black background, with grey markers and unpaired red/green pixels preserved",
  "transform": "recolor each domino's green pixel to teal and recolor its red pixel to bg"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 3-7 rows x 3-9 cols, black background
  colors: exactly {black, red, green, grey} in input; output adds teal
  dominoes:
    count: 1-3 red-green 4-connected pairs per grid
    orientation: horizontal or vertical, mixed within a grid [observed]
    each green in a domino touches exactly 1 red; each red touches exactly 1 green
  distractors:
    unpaired_red: 0-3 isolated red pixels (no adjacent green)
    unpaired_green: 0-2 isolated green pixels (no adjacent red)
  grey_markers: 1-5 isolated grey pixels, preserved unchanged in output
  all_pixels_sparse: all non-black pixels are single cells; density ~19-33%
  adjacency: unpaired red/green may be adjacent to grey but never to the opposite color
  transform:
    for each domino - green cell becomes teal, red cell becomes black
    all other pixels (grey, unpaired red, unpaired green) unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct placement (constraints are simple enough)
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size H in [3,7], W in [3,9]
    2. Pick domino count D in [1,3]
    3. Place D dominoes - for each, pick an empty cell for green, pick an adjacent empty cell for red; verify no red/green adjacency conflicts (each red touches only its paired green and vice versa)
    4. Pick counts for unpaired red (0-3), unpaired green (0-2), grey (1-5)
    5. Place each unpaired pixel on an empty cell; unpaired red must have no adjacent green, unpaired green must have no adjacent red; grey has no color constraint but must not create red-green adjacency
    6. Verify overall density is reasonable (~15-35% non-black)
```
